# lerobot_nanowm_aug

LeRobot (PyTorch robotics library) with an in-tree research prototype that uses NanoWM
imagined rollouts as data augmentation for a PushT diffusion policy
(`examples/nanowm_pusht_aug/`). See `AGENTS.md`, `AGENT_GUIDE.md` and
`examples/nanowm_pusht_aug/README.md` for details.

## Tech stack

Python 3.12+ · PyTorch · Hugging Face (datasets, Hub, accelerate) · draccus (config/CLI) · Gymnasium · uv

## Commands

- Always use `uv run` instead of raw `python` / `pip`.
- Setup: `uv sync --locked --extra test --extra dev`
- Tests: `uv run pytest tests -svv --maxfail=10`
- Lint/format: `pre-commit run --all-files`

## Long-running tasks: use tmux (SSH-disconnect safe)

After Plan mode, when executing the plan, run every task in a tmux session so it keeps
running if the SSH connection drops. This applies to training, eval, data generation,
and any command that may take more than a minute.

- Start detached, one named session per task:
  `tmux new-session -d -s <task_name> 'cd /home/feng-en.yeh/repos/lerobot_nanowm_aug && <command> 2>&1 | tee logs/<task_name>.log'`
- Check progress: `tmux capture-pane -pt <task_name> -S -50` or `tail -n 50 logs/<task_name>.log`
- List sessions: `tmux ls`; attach: `tmux attach -t <task_name>`; stop: `tmux kill-session -t <task_name>`
- Reuse an existing session name only after confirming it is finished (`tmux ls`).
- Do not run long tasks directly in the foreground agent shell; poll the tmux session/log instead.
- Quick commands (git, ls, small tests, lint) may still run directly.

### Progress check every 30 minutes

After launching a long-running task in tmux, use the `/loop` skill to check progress every 30 minutes
(e.g. `/loop 30m check tmux session <task_name>`). Each check should:

- Confirm the session is alive (`tmux ls`) and read the latest log lines (`tail -n 50 logs/<task_name>.log`).
- Report step/epoch, loss or key metrics, and any errors, NaNs, or stalls (log not updated since last check).
- If the task crashed or stalled, report the cause and propose a fix; do not restart it silently.
- When the task finishes, summarize the results and stop the loop.

## Code layout (`src/lerobot/`)

- `scripts/` CLI entry points (`lerobot-train`, `lerobot-eval`, `lerobot-record`)
- `configs/` draccus dataclass configs (`@register_subclass` for polymorphism)
- `policies/` one subdir per policy, inheriting `PreTrainedPolicy`; lazy imports in `factory.py`
- `processor/`, `datasets/`, `envs/`, `robots/`, `motors/`, `cameras/`, `teleoperators/`

## Conventions

- Prefer top-level imports; relative imports between siblings in a module, absolute across modules.
- Optional deps: guard with `TYPE_CHECKING or _foo_available` plus `require_package(...)` at use time;
  reuse flags in `utils/import_utils.py`, never call `is_package_available` directly.
- Mypy is strict only for `envs`, `configs`, `optim`, `model`, `cameras`, `motors`, `transport`:
  add type annotations when touching these.

## NanoWM augmentation prototype (`examples/nanowm_pusht_aug/`)

- Two separate uv venvs that cannot be merged:
  - lerobot venv: Python >= 3.12 (`datagen/`, `eval/`, `scripts/`)
  - nanowm venv: `third_party/nano-world-model/.venv`, Python 3.11 (`imagined_rollout.py`)
- The pipeline crosses venvs only through `.npz` files on disk.
- `datagen/action_convert.py` and `datagen/coverage_from_frame.py` are imported from both venvs:
  keep them pure numpy/cv2 and never import `lerobot` there.
- Do not rename `datagen/` to `utils/` (collides with nano-world-model's top-level `src/utils`).
- `third_party/nano-world-model` is a vendored squashed git subtree: do not edit it in place;
  update via `git subtree pull`.
- Generated dirs (`imagined/`, `cl/`, `diag/`, `logs/`, `outputs/`) are git-ignored and reproducible.
- Image-only policy: PushT state contains block pose, so it is not reconstructable from actions.
- Keep real and imagined data at the same fps (sub-sample real to fps 2, `frame_interval=5`)
  and use identical policy hyperparameters for the `a` vs `a+aug` comparison.
